This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Notably, our investigation confirms the validity of the condition $\beta<\frac{\mu+4}{4}$ in ensuring the normal ground state solution is an isolated critical point for the original Choquard equation when $\lambda=0$. Additionally, we demonstrate the applicability of this condition to the more general biharmonic Choquard type equation when additional constraints are imposed. Our findings are further substantiated by a practical computation involving the Krein-Rabinowitz variational principle. Lastly, our research is extended to encompass equations defined on bounded and locally unbounded domains in $\mathbb{R}^4$. Requiremnts are discussed for the equation's coefficients, and the existence of a normalized ground state solution is demonstrated through an application of the homotopy-based minimax principle and the mountain league theorem. Notably, our study confirms the validity of an earlier conjecture proposed by Gao et al. when the domain is bounded, and it further generalizes that conjecture to a broader context. Last but not least, our research is extended to encompass equations defined on bounded and locally unbounded domains in $\mathbb{R}^4$. Requirements for the equation's coefficients are discussed, and the existence of a normalized ground state solution is demonstrated through an application of the homotopy-based minimax principle and the mountain league theorem. Notably, our study confirms the validity of an earlier conjecture proposed by Gao et al. when the domain is bounded, and it further generalizes that conjecture to a broader context.

This paper delves into the source of high false positive rates in Visual Relationship Detection (VRD). During training, we discovered that the relationship proposal distribution is skewed, with the majority of incorrect relationships being easily identified due to poor object detection. As a result, low-frequency difficult proposals struggle to contribute effectively. Our proposed framework, Spatially-Aware Balanced Negative Proposal Sampling (SABRA), mitigates the impact of false positives in VRD tasks. To counter the imbalance, SABRA incorporates Balanced Negative Proposal Sampling (BNPS), which divides proposals into distinct sub-classes based on difficulty and samples a balanced distribution accordingly. By employing this strategy, BNPS yields an optimized model performance and a notable decrease in false positives. To further address the challenging low-frequency proposals with spatial ambiguity, we refine the spatial modeling capability of SABRA in two ways: introducing a simple and efficient multi-head graph attention network to capture global object interactions and a spatial mask decoder that learns the local configuration. Our approach demonstrates superior performance compared to the current state-of-the-art methods across three datasets for human-object interaction and visual relationship detection. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/zeze3626/SABRA. Our benchmark page is accessible at https://zeze3626.github.io/SABRA/.},

